# Worker Service Environment Variables
# Copy this file to .env and update with your actual values

## Core Infrastructure
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3003

## Database
# worker-service connects as telemetry_worker_app, NOT the shared telemetry_app, and the reason
# is the nightly invoice job (T-042).
#
# Every other query this service makes is tenant-scoped and runs under RLS. The invoice job
# cannot be: to answer "which tenants had unbilled usage yesterday" it has to read across every
# tenant at once, which the v1_0 policies forbid by design. Measured as telemetry_app with no
# tenant context, SELECT DISTINCT "tenantId" FROM "UsageLine" WHERE billed = false returns zero
# rows -- so the exception had to be built rather than documented.
#
# prisma/migrations/v1_7_worker_billing_enumerator builds it: one SECURITY DEFINER resolver
# returning tenant ids and nothing else, owned by a NOLOGIN role that reads past the UsageLine
# policy through one targeted FOR SELECT policy rather than through the BYPASSRLS role
# attribute, with EXECUTE granted to telemetry_worker_app ALONE -- revoked from PUBLIC and from
# telemetry_app, which gateway, usage-, billing- and analytics-service share. Granting it to the
# shared role would hand all four an enumerate-every-tenant oracle; that is the whole reason
# worker gets a fourth role instead of a fourth grant.
#
# This role is still NOSUPERUSER and NOBYPASSRLS, owns no table, and holds DML on exactly "Event"
# and "UsageLine" -- no DELETE, no grant on "Tenant" (referential integrity does not consult the
# calling role's privileges; measured), and no blanket default grant, so a future table has to be
# granted deliberately.
#
# ORDERING ON DEPLOY: apply and verify v1_7 BEFORE pointing a worker at this role. The flip must
# never precede the grant. See docs/releases/t-042-worker-billing-enumerator.md.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://telemetry_worker_app:telemetry_worker_app_local_dev@localhost:5432/telemetry

## Cache
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

## Billing (T-042)
# Base URL of billing-service, which the nightly invoice job POSTs to at
# /v1/internal/billing/generate carrying INTERNAL_API_SECRET. Required with no default: the env
# schema throws at module load, so a worker with no billing address fails to start rather than
# discovering the problem at 02:00 UTC on a path that raises no alarm.
#
# Validated with z.string().url(), which -- measured on zod 3.25.76 -- delegates to new URL() and
# so accepts any scheme: "billing-service:3004" parses, with protocol "billing-service:". It
# rejects a relative or malformed value. Include the scheme.
BILLING_SERVICE_URL=http://localhost:3004

## Observability
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318
LOG_LEVEL=debug

## Security (SENSITIVE - change in production)
# Shared service-to-service secret. Required: startup fails if it is missing, or under 32
# characters once leading/trailing whitespace is stripped (the schema trims before measuring).
# The gateway does not currently proxy to worker-service; this guards /v1/internal/worker/*
# against in-cluster callers and the published 3003 port mapping.
INTERNAL_API_SECRET=dev-local-secret-change-in-production

## Redis Streams (consumer side)
# All five are now read (src/events/stream.consumer.ts): REDIS_STREAM_NAME and
# REDIS_CONSUMER_GROUP by T-038's XGROUP CREATE at startup, and REDIS_CONSUMER_NAME,
# STREAM_BLOCK_MS and STREAM_BATCH_SIZE by T-039's XREADGROUP loop and XAUTOCLAIM recovery.
#
# Because the bootstrap is fail-closed, the worker no longer starts without a reachable
# Redis: any XGROUP reply other than BUSYGROUP propagates and the process exits non-zero.
# Pointed at a port nothing listens on, this was observed to fail in ~160 ms with
# MaxRetriesPerRequestError rather than hanging.
#
# REDIS_STREAM_NAME must match usage-service's REDIS_STREAM_NAME. It is the stream the producer
# XADDs to; a consumer pointed anywhere else will block on XREADGROUP forever and still report
# healthy. The two *defaults* are now one constant
# (EVENT_STREAM_CONSTANTS.USAGE_EVENTS_STREAM in @telemetry/shared-types), so renaming it moves
# both and reddens both suites - but re-pinning one site to a literal still diverges silently,
# and this override is per-service: set it on both services or neither.
REDIS_STREAM_NAME=telemetry:events
# Consumer group the worker creates on startup (T-038). One group per logical consumer fleet.
# Created with MKSTREAM at `$`, so the group only ever sees entries XADDed after it exists:
# the two entries already on telemetry:events at the time T-038 landed carry a tenantId that
# is absent from the Tenant table, and Event.tenantId has a foreign key to Tenant(id)
# (Event_tenantId_fkey), so T-040 could not have stored them - the insert is rejected with
# foreign_key_violation. Verify with `xinfo groups telemetry:events` after a first run.
# To undo a bootstrap: `redis-cli XGROUP DESTROY telemetry:events worker-group`.
#
# MKSTREAM has a cost worth knowing before you edit REDIS_STREAM_NAME above: it creates the
# key if it is missing, so a *typo* in the stream name does not fail. The worker replies OK,
# creates a new empty stream under the wrong name, puts a group on it, and reports healthy on
# /health while consuming nothing - the same silent failure the REDIS_STREAM_NAME note warns
# about, now reachable without the producer ever having written anything. MKSTREAM is still
# required (a fresh environment has no stream at all). After changing either name, check
# `redis-cli XLEN <name>` and `redis-cli XINFO GROUPS <name>` rather than trusting startup.
REDIS_CONSUMER_GROUP=worker-group
# Identifies this worker instance within the group; must be unique per running instance.
#
# Leave it unset in almost every case. The default is derived at startup as
# <hostname>-<pid>, which satisfies the uniqueness requirement above by itself - including for
# several workers on one host, which is the local and docker-compose shape. It was the fixed
# literal `worker-1` until T-043, which is to say the default contradicted this line.
#
# Uniqueness is not cosmetic. On a clean shutdown the worker deregisters its own name with
# XGROUP DELCONSUMER, and that command DESTROYS any entries the named consumer still holds
# unacknowledged - measured on Redis 7.0.15: two pending entries, DELCONSUMER returned 2,
# XPENDING dropped to 0 and neither XAUTOCLAIM nor XREADGROUP could reach them again, while
# XLEN still showed 2. The worker guards this by deregistering only when its own row reports
# `pending 0`, but two instances SHARING a name share that row, so the guard reads one
# instance's zero while the other is holding work. Setting the same value here on two
# instances re-opens exactly that hole.
#
# If you do set it, make it unique per running instance - not per deployment. Inspect the
# names in use with `redis-cli XINFO CONSUMERS telemetry:events worker-group`.
#
# The example below carries NO value, and that is the point. It was `worker-1` - the exact value
# the paragraph above describes as causing unrecoverable loss - so `cp .env.example .env` plus
# uncommenting handed you the hole on the second replica.
#
# An empty value fails startup rather than starting wrong, and that is a mechanism rather than a
# convention: REDIS_CONSUMER_NAME is `z.string().min(1)` in src/config/env.ts, and parseEnv throws
# at module load. Measured against the real schema (zod 3.25.76):
#
#   ""                                           -> REJECTED, "String must contain at least 1 character(s)"
#   "worker-1"                                   -> accepted
#   "<unique-per-instance, e.g. worker-$HOSTNAME-$$>" -> ACCEPTED, verbatim
#
# The third row is why this is empty and not a descriptive placeholder: nothing expands a
# placeholder, so an operator who uncommented one would start cleanly under a shared literal
# name - the same hole, wearing a label that says it is not one.
#REDIS_CONSUMER_NAME=
# How long a single XREADGROUP call will block waiting for entries, in milliseconds (T-039).
#
# It does NOT add to shutdown time, and that is still true after T-043's drain. The loop reads on
# a connection of its own, and shutdown disconnects that connection before closing the app: a
# disconnect was measured ending an in-flight BLOCK 5000 read in 204 ms (re-derived at T-043;
# the earlier figure was 205 ms), where letting the app's own quit() run first waited it out for
# 4883 ms. Raising this is therefore cheap for deploys - it costs redelivery latency on the
# *next* entry after a stop, not shutdown latency.
#
# What DOES bound shutdown is the drain: stop() disconnects this read, then waits for whatever
# the message handler has in hand, up to WORKER_SHUTDOWN.DRAIN_TIMEOUT_MS (3000 ms, a constant -
# not an operator setting). That wait is driven by handler duration, not by this value.
#
# It also sets the startup reclaim threshold, which is this value x 2: an entry another
# consumer holds is only reclaimed once it has been idle for longer than that. Setting this
# very low makes a worker more willing to steal work a live peer is still doing; setting it
# very high delays recovery of work a crashed peer abandoned.
STREAM_BLOCK_MS=5000
# XREADGROUP COUNT - entries a single iteration will claim (T-039). Bounded 1..100, an
# operational ceiling chosen to mirror the batch cap usage-service enforces
# (INGESTION_CONSTANTS.BATCH_SIZE_MAX), not a Redis protocol limit.
#
# Also the page size for startup recovery. XAUTOCLAIM is paginated, and the loop follows the
# cursor to the end, so this changes the number of round trips a restart costs rather than how
# much work it recovers: 5 pending entries at COUNT 2 were observed needing three rounds.
STREAM_BATCH_SIZE=10

## Dead-letter handling (T-041, Q10)
# How many times one stream entry may fail before it is copied to DEAD_LETTER_STREAM and
# acknowledged. Bounded 1..10. The attempts are NOT immediate: an entry that failed is only
# offered again once XAUTOCLAIM will take it back, which needs STREAM_BLOCK_MS x 2 of idleness,
# so the default budget of 3 spans roughly 20-30 seconds at STREAM_BLOCK_MS=5000 rather than
# three round trips. That is why Q10 needed no separate retry-delay setting - raising
# STREAM_BLOCK_MS above lengthens the retry spacing as well as the read block.
#
# Trade-off, stated rather than implied: a value of 1 dead-letters on the first failure,
# including a transient database blip, so good usage data goes to the dead-letter queue instead
# of being billed. The cap of 10 buys roughly two minutes of retrying at the default block
# interval, which still will not survive a multi-minute outage. Replaying out of the
# dead-letter stream is not implemented.
MAX_RETRY_COUNT=3
# Redis stream an entry is copied to once its retry budget is spent. Must NOT be
# REDIS_STREAM_NAME: a dead letter written back onto the source stream is redelivered, fails
# again, and dead-letters itself forever. This is enforced, not advisory -- the env schema
# rejects the collision at startup (T-041, decision M-6), so a worker configured this way does
# not boot.
#
# Nothing trims this stream and nothing watches it, and there are TWO consequences, not one.
#
# Growth: no MAXLEN, so the stream only ever gets longer. Retention: the source stream trims at
# MAXLEN ~ 100000, so a customer payload ages out of Redis there - and a dead-lettered copy of
# one never does. Every record holds the whole original field list including tenantId and all
# flattened customer metadata, indefinitely. That is deliberate (the record is the only surviving
# copy and has to be replayable), but it means the dead-letter stream is the one place in this
# service where customer data has unbounded retention. Treat XLEN telemetry:dead-letter as a data
# ledger, not just a health number. A retention lever belongs with T-057's replay work; until
# then, draining it is manual (XRANGE to read, XDEL or XTRIM to remove - and removing is data
# loss, so read first).
#
# There is no metric and no alert until T-057,
# so the operational lever is `redis-cli XLEN telemetry:dead-letter` - a non-zero length means
# usage events were dropped from the billing path, and the record is the only copy left. Read a
# record with `redis-cli XRANGE telemetry:dead-letter - + COUNT 10`; each carries originalId,
# streamName, groupName, payload (the original field list, JSON-encoded), failureReason,
# failedAt and retryCount. The payload is stored because a pending entry's data can be evicted
# from the source stream by MAXLEN while its id stays pending - measured, so an id-only record
# would be unreplayable.
DEAD_LETTER_STREAM=telemetry:dead-letter
